The projection \(j_\infty\) from the function space \([D_\infty \to D_\infty]\) onto the inverse limit \(D_\infty\) is the supremum of the Scott maps `jInfTerm n`. `projInfInf.apply` says that evaluating it at a Scott map \(f\) returns the supremum over \(n\) of the values of those summands. `projInfInf.preservesDirectedSup` says it preserves directed suprema, because a bundled Scott map is Scott-continuous.

`projInfInf.embInfInf_eq` says that applying \(j_\infty\) to \(i_\infty(x)\) returns the supremum over \(n\) of the level-\((n+1)\) embeddings of the components \(x_{n+1}\). `projInfInf.comp_embInfInf` says the composite of the projection with the embedding is the identity Scott map on \(D_\infty\). `projInfInf.embInfInf` is that identity evaluated at an element.
